<!--block:B0009-->
\section{Introduction}

<!--block:B0010-->
Three-disk scattering is a canonical open hyperbolic system: a point particle moves freely outside three disjoint convex disks and reflects specularly at their boundaries. In the no-eclipse regime, trapped trajectories admit a finite symbolic coding with no immediate repetition of a disk label. That coding represents a return map. The continuous-time physical flow is a suspension whose roof is Euclidean distance between successive collisions. Confusing the unit symbolic clock with the physical clock can turn an exact matrix identity into a false flow determinant.

<!--block:B0011-->
% SOURCE: Ikawa1988, Introduction and theorem statements, for periodic rays and Poincare maps in exterior scattering.
Periodic rays and their Poincaré maps are central to rigorous analysis of wave decay outside convex bodies \citep[Introduction and theorem statements]{Ikawa1988}.
% SOURCE: GaspardRice1989Semiclassical, Sections II--IV, for periodic-orbit semiclassical construction.
In the three-disk literature, periodic-orbit and semiclassical constructions organize resonances of a chaotic repellor \citep[Secs.~II--IV]{GaspardRice1989Semiclassical}.
% SOURCE: GaspardRice1989Exact, abstract and Sections II--III, for exact multiple scattering.
Exact quantum scattering, however, is expressed through a multiple-scattering matrix and its determinant, not by declaring the symbolic adjacency determinant to be the physical scattering determinant \citep[abstract and Secs.~II--III]{GaspardRice1989Exact}. The distinction between exact, semiclassical, and symbolic objects is mathematical, not terminological.

<!--block:B0012-->
This paper asks:
\begin{quote}
\emph{Can the exact determinant of the unit-roof no-repeat shift be transferred to the physical three-disk flow by a scalar substitution \(z=e^{-cs}\) that preserves primitive owners and repetitions?}
\end{quote}

<!--block:B0013-->
We answer no. Two symmetric periodic orbits already have different physical mean roofs. The bouncing orbit between two disks has mean \(d-2a\); the triangular orbit visiting all three has mean \(d-\sqrt3a\). Since their difference is \((2-\sqrt3)a>0\), the physical roof is not cohomologous to a constant. A scalar substitution assigns length \(cn\) to every period-\(n\) symbolic owner and cannot reproduce both witnesses. The same calculation gives the sharp two-witness minimax lower bound \((2-\sqrt3)a/2\).

<!--block:B0111-->
The three-disk-specific contribution is bounded accordingly. It is not a new general cohomology criterion, symbolic determinant theorem, or exact scattering construction. It is an owner- and repetition-preserving audit of the proposed scalar bridge: two exact geometric witnesses settle nontransfer, the minimax bound quantifies its unavoidable error, and the object-typing analysis prevents symbolic, semiclassical, and exact-scattering credit from being reassigned to the physical flow.

<!--block:B0014-->
The result does not diminish the exact symbolic theorem. For \(q\ge2\), the adjacency matrix \(A_q=J_q-I_q\) has explicit spectrum, trace formula, primitive count, and Euler determinant. Likewise, every real \(2\times2\) symplectic hyperbolic return map has a universal half-density factorization. These positive results belong to different typed objects. The first concerns a unit-roof suspension; the second is a local stability identity. Neither supplies a nonconstant-roof transfer operator, convergence theory, exact multiple-scattering determinant, arithmetic source, or spectral identification for the physical flow.

<!--block:B0015-->
The finite computation is used only for solver and reproducibility validation. It replays 747 oriented primitive symbolic owners through length twelve at each of three neighboring geometries, for 2,241 physical-orbit rows, verifies the exact witness formulas in the locked implementation, and checks that the period-two calibration disagrees with the other 744 owners at each geometry. These row counts audit the frozen owner convention, solver output, serialization, and deterministic rebuild. They are not additional proof of noncohomology and do not increase the theorem's scope beyond the two exact symmetric owners.

<!--block:B0016-->
No prime sequence, zeta-zero list, or resonance target is used in designing or evaluating the statistic. The physical Route-A tuple remains unassigned, and no alternate operator route is invoked. The contribution is a negative-control paper that proves where the tempting scalar bridge fails and records what a genuine physical-flow construction still needs.

<!--block:B0017-->
\subsection{Research logic and claim hierarchy}

<!--block:B0018-->
The analysis proceeds in an order intended to prevent credit transfer between unlike objects. Table~\ref{tab:object-map} distinguishes the physical billiard flow, the unit-roof symbolic suspension, a semiclassical cycle construction, and the exact boundary-channel multiple-scattering determinant by state space, owner, clock or weight, determinant status, and permitted relation. The paper first proves statements internal to the first two relevant types: the no-repeat adjacency determinant for a unit roof and the local half-density formula for a symplectic return map. It then tests only the owner- and repetition-preserving scalar bridge to the continuous physical clock; no equality among the four objects is assumed.

<!--block:B0019-->
This order separates an algebraic success from a modeling success. The adjacency determinant exactly counts closed symbolic walks, and Möbius inversion exactly recovers primitive symbolic owners. Those facts establish internal symbolic ownership. They do not establish that symbolic length equals Euclidean time. Similarly, the half-density exactly factors a local stability amplitude, but says nothing by itself about a global Euler product, its analytic continuation, or a quantum resonance set.

<!--block:B0020-->
The central negative theorem is not based on poor numerical agreement. It identifies an invariant obstruction: constant-cohomologous roofs have the same mean on every periodic owner, while two explicit physical owners have unequal means. The finite replay is downstream. It demonstrates the practical scale of the mismatch under a locked cutoff and solver, but the conclusion survives if every non-symmetric numerical row is removed.

<!--block:B0021-->
This hierarchy determines the evidence language. Exact symbolic identities and geometric symmetric periods are proved. High-precision orbit solutions are numerically certified at the frozen cutoff. The absence of an arithmetic source is a modeling choice. The physical determinant and physical Route-A tuple are unassigned. None of these labels is promoted by resemblance to a resonance plot.

<!--block:B0022-->
\section{Related work, background, and three distinct objects}

<!--block:B0023-->
\subsection{The physical billiard flow}

<!--block:B0024-->
Let \(D_0,D_1,D_2\subset\mathbb R^2\) be closed disks of radius \(a>0\), with centers \(C_0,C_1,C_2\) forming an equilateral triangle of side \(d\). The exterior billiard domain is
\(\Omega=\mathbb R^2\setminus(D_0\cup D_1\cup D_2)\). A trapped trajectory travels at unit speed and reflects with equal incoming and outgoing angles. Its primitive owner is an oriented cyclic collision word, provided the physical orbit exists and is primitive; its clock is total flight length \(T_p\); its \(r\)-fold repetition has time \(rT_p\).

<!--block:B0025-->
The equilateral configuration is no-eclipse whenever \(d>4a/\sqrt3\).
Indeed, the convex hull of two radius-\(a\) disks is the radius-\(a\)
capsule around their center segment, while the third center lies at distance
\(\sqrt3d/2>2a\) from that segment.  Hence the third disk is disjoint from
the capsule.  The three frozen geometries \(d/a=29/5,6,31/5\) all satisfy
this condition.

<!--block:B0026-->
The nearest general cohomological framework is the periodic-orbit obstruction for hyperbolic dynamics: if a roof is cohomologous to a constant, every periodic owner has that same mean roof. We use only this necessary telescoping direction, not a new Liv\v{s}ic theorem \citep[main cohomology criteria]{Livsic1972}.%<!--ref:Livsic1972--><!--anchor:section:main-cohomology-criteria-->
The reciprocal determinant for a finite-type shift is likewise classical \citep[pp.~43--49]{BowenLanford1970},%<!--ref:BowenLanford1970--><!--anchor:page:43-49-->
while flow-zeta and transfer-operator formulations retain the roof and dynamical weights \citep[Secs.~1--3]{Ruelle1976}.%<!--ref:Ruelle1976--><!--anchor:section:1-3-->
For open billiards, the established framework distinguishes symbolic itineraries, classical stability, semiclassical cycle expansions, and exact quantum multiple scattering \citep[Secs.~2--6]{Wirzba1999}.%<!--ref:Wirzba1999--><!--anchor:section:2-6-->
Against that background, the manuscript contributes neither the general cohomology obstruction nor the established determinant theories. Its narrower increment is the exact equilateral two-witness test and minimax bound for one owner-preserving scalar-transfer proposal, together with a typed finite replay that validates the implementation without transferring symbolic or semiclassical credit to the physical flow.

<!--block:B0027-->
\subsection{The no-repeat shift and unit-roof suspension}

<!--block:B0028-->
Let
\[
 \Sigma_q=\{x\in\{0,\ldots,q-1\}^{\mathbb Z}:x_{k+1}\ne x_k\}
\]
with left shift \(\sigma\). Its adjacency matrix is \(A_q=J_q-I_q\), where \(J_q\) is the all-ones matrix. A length-\(n\) closed word is counted by \(\tr(A_q^n)\). The unit-roof suspension assigns time \(n\) to every length-\(n\) word, independently of geometry.

<!--block:B0029-->
% SOURCE: BowenLanford1970, pages 43--49, for shift zeta functions and adjacency determinants.
The reciprocal-determinant form of a finite-type shift zeta function is classical \citep[pp.~43--49]{BowenLanford1970}.
% SOURCE: Ruelle1976, Sections 1--3, for expanding-map and flow zeta functions.
For flows, roof and dynamical weights are part of the object, and transfer-operator constructions retain timing information \citep[Secs.~1--3]{Ruelle1976}. The symbolic return graph is necessary coding data, not permission to replace every flight length by one.

<!--block:B0030-->
\subsection{Exact quantum scattering}

<!--block:B0031-->
The exact quantum problem solves Helmholtz or Schrödinger scattering with disk boundary conditions. Its determinant arises from a multiple-scattering operator or matrix, with angular-momentum channels and geometric translation kernels.
% SOURCE: GaspardRice1989Exact, abstract and Sections II--III, for exact S-matrix quantization.
The exact three-hard-disk \(S\)-matrix formulation identifies resonances through the appropriate multiple-scattering determinant \citep[abstract and Secs.~II--III]{GaspardRice1989Exact}.
% SOURCE: Wirzba1999, Sections 4--6, for multiscattering determinant and semiclassical limits.
Systematic treatment emphasizes both determinant structure and care with semiclassical and cumulant limits \citep[Secs.~4--6]{Wirzba1999}. The polynomial \(\det(I-zA_3)\) below is a symbolic determinant, not the exact quantum object.

