The machine-readable Stage-4 lock at \texttt{experiments/stage4\_reproducibility\_lock.json} pins CPython 3.12.3, NumPy 2.4.4, SciPy 1.16.1, mpmath 1.3.0, the platform assumptions, the current bibliography digest, and a closed 68-file inventory of Round-2--8 sources, tests, inputs, outputs, receipts, and reproduction commands. The matching \texttt{experiments/stage4\_reproducibility\_receipt.json} records the successful validation, and \texttt{bash experiments/reproduce\_stage4.sh} performs a read-only replay with no canonical-refresh path.

<!--block:B0083-->
The replay uses no prime data, zeta zeros, or scattering-resonance table. Its purpose is clock ownership, not external-target optimization. Center-polygon proxy lengths remain distinct from solved billiard lengths and are never called physical orbits.

<!--block:B0084-->
\subsection{Robustness interpretation}

<!--block:B0085-->
The three neighboring geometries exercise the solver away from one parameter value and show that the mismatch pattern is stable under modest separation changes. They do not supply the proof of genericity, because \cref{cor:gap} already gives a \(d\)-independent gap wherever the symmetric orbits are admissible. Nor do they count as three independent arithmetic systems; all share the same source-absent billiard type.

<!--block:B0086-->
Shuffled, random, and composite controls from earlier half-density work have a different purpose. They show that persistence of the leading hyperbolic amplitude is generic and therefore unsuitable as arithmetic evidence. They are not used to claim that physical orbits are random. The conclusion is stop-scoped: the tested amplitude cannot carry the proposed specificity, while its analytic factorization remains valid.

<!--block:B0087-->
Conditioning is separated from substance. Difficult Newton rows might bias a finite comparison if discarded. Recording and retaining them closes that procedural loophole. It does not convert numerical coordinates into interval proofs and does not address the infinite-cutoff question.

<!--block:B0088-->
\section{Adversarial and Route-A assessment}

<!--block:B0089-->
\subsection{Why symbolic success cannot be inherited}

<!--block:B0090-->
In the object map, only the unit-roof symbolic suspension owns the exact adjacency determinant. Its exact primitive ownership and analytic determinant give the typed tuple
\[
 (A0_{\mathrm{fail}},A1_{\mathrm{pass\ analytic}},
 A2_{\mathrm{analytic\ determinant}},A3_{\mathrm{fail}},A4_{\mathrm{fail}}).
\]
The arithmetic route is rejected because no arithmetic source exists by construction, collision parity is merely \(z\mapsto-z\), and no arithmetic Euler factor or spectral target is identified. These A1/A2 credits belong only to the unit-roof object; the semiclassical and exact quantum rows in Table~\ref{tab:object-map} do not transfer them to the physical flow.

<!--block:B0091-->
The physical flow has a different owner-weight pair: oriented billiard owners carry the nonconstant Euclidean roof \(T_p\). \Cref{thm:nontransfer} refutes only the owner- and repetition-preserving global scalar bridge, so the physical Route-A tuple remains unassigned. It would be wrong to copy symbolic A2, a semiclassical approximation, or the exact boundary-channel determinant into the physical row. A positive physical construction would require a nonconstant-roof operator or flow zeta with a stated function space, domain, and convergence result, followed by a controlled comparison with the separately defined semiclassical and exact quantum objects.

<!--block:B0092-->
\subsection{Adversarial controls}

<!--block:B0093-->
\begin{itemize}
 \item The half-density theorem holds for every real two-dimensional symplectic hyperbolic map of the stated type; persistence under neighboring parameters proves too much to be arithmetic evidence.
 \item The phase identity holds for every alphabet size \(q\ge2\); it is a substitution symmetry, not a three-disk arithmetic signature.
 \item The exact clock gap persists across the equilateral geometry family and falsifies the scalar bridge.
 \item Exact quantum scattering uses a multiple-scattering determinant; equality is not inferred from shared resonance language.
 \item No target labels enter statistic design, solver selection, or replay.
\end{itemize}

<!--block:B0094-->
No alternative self-adjoint spectral operator is invoked. No operator domain, boundary condition, self-adjointness proof, spectral reality theorem, or target-zero correspondence is supplied.

<!--block:B0095-->
\subsection{What would change the physical-flow status}

<!--block:B0096-->
A positive successor would first define a nonconstant roof on the no-repeat shift from physical flight geometry and prove that its periodic sums agree with billiard periods on a declared owner domain. It would next construct an operator or dynamical zeta using this roof, state its function space and analytic domain, and prove the relevant determinant or continuation statement. Only then could it compare a controlled semiclassical limit with the separately defined multiple-scattering determinant.

<!--block:B0097-->
If an arithmetic interpretation were also sought, an independent arithmetic source and owner map would be required before target evaluation. The source-derived phase would need conjugacy, orientation, and repetition laws, and it would have to fail prespecified source-removed controls. Agreement with a finite resonance table could then be a downstream test but could not define the phase.

<!--block:B0098-->
Shorter developments can still be useful. Interval certification of the two witness coordinates would strengthen the artifact layer, although analytic geometry already proves their lengths. A rigorous Hölder bound for the physical roof or a pressure computation with explicit error would advance the physical-flow object. Neither result by itself supplies arithmetic A0 or a spectral zero correspondence.

<!--block:B0099-->
The ordering of these tasks prevents a familiar logical shortcut. A refined numerical resonance match cannot retroactively define the physical clock, and an exact symbolic determinant cannot supply a missing arithmetic source. Ownership and timing must be established first, analytic wiring second, and target evaluation last. This paper completes the negative test at the timing step and leaves the later steps open.

<!--block:B0100-->
\section{Limitations and open problems}

<!--block:B0101-->
First, the theorem rules out a constant scalar clock, not all symbolic descriptions of the physical flow. A Hölder nonconstant roof is natural, and a transfer operator weighted by \(e^{-s\tau}\) may encode classical flow data. Its functional setting, analytic continuation, and relation to physical or semiclassical quantities remain open.

<!--block:B0102-->
Second, the 2,241-row ledger is finite. It validates frozen words through length twelve, solver behavior, serialization, and deterministic rebuilding, but not asymptotic cycle-expansion convergence, an infinite primitive-owner census, or the full trapped set. The two-orbit proof avoids this finite-cutoff limitation for noncohomology; the replay is reproducibility evidence for the implementation only.

<!--block:B0103-->
Third, \cref{thm:half} concerns a linearized return map. Global amplitudes may include Maslov phases, curvature, pruning in other geometries, and quantum diffraction or channel effects. The universal leading half-density cannot decide them.

<!--block:B0104-->
Fourth, no arithmetic source is assigned to the three-disk control. This is a modeling choice fixed before computation, not an empirical finding. The paper cannot claim an arithmetic Euler product, prime-owner map, or Hilbert--Pólya realization, and it does not fit resonances or zeros.

<!--block:B0105-->
Fifth, the exact multiple-scattering determinant and a classical nonconstant-roof product are distinct even when semiclassical theory relates their expansions. This manuscript does not construct the latter product, establish its functional setting or analytic continuation, or prove a controlled approximation to the former. Thus ``remain open'' in the first limitation means unresolved within this manuscript, not an assertion about the field's global open-problem status. Any future comparison would require explicit limits and estimates; no equality is claimed here.

<!--block:B0106-->
Finally, orientation and symmetry reduction change owner multiplicities. Our theorem uses oriented cyclic owners and the physical replay preserves that convention. Other quotients must rederive primitive and repetition bookkeeping.

<!--block:B0107-->
\section{Conclusion}

<!--block:B0108-->
An exact unit-roof symbolic determinant does not become a physical-flow determinant through one exponential scalar clock. In equilateral three-disk scattering, the period-two and period-three mean flight lengths differ by \((2-\sqrt3)a\). The two exact owners prove that the physical roof is not cohomologous to a constant, exclude every owner- and repetition-preserving substitution \(z=e^{-cs}\), and give the sharp two-witness minimax bound. This is the manuscript's theorem-level three-disk increment within the established cohomology, symbolic-zeta, semiclassical, and multiple-scattering frameworks.

<!--block:B0116-->
The four-object map keeps the remaining results in their proper types. The \(q\)-symbol determinant and hyperbolic half-density are exact positive statements for the unit-roof symbolic and local stability objects, respectively; neither supplies a physical or quantum determinant. The 2,241-row replay validates the frozen solver, owner convention, serialization, and deterministic rebuild only and is not additional proof of noncohomology. A successor physical construction would need a genuine nonconstant-roof operator with explicit ownership and analytic control before any controlled comparison with semiclassical or exact quantum scattering.

